Potential # Closed non-displaced clavicle fracture from fall onto outstretched hand to shoulder
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Focal mid-shaft clavicle tenderness, Arm supported by contralateral hand, FOOSH mechanism without step-off deformity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-043
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe collarbone pain after a fall, requiring urgent clinical clearance of major displacement, pneumothorax, or neurovascular injury.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a FOOSH mechanism resulting in focal clavicle pain and inability to use the arm. Urgent bedside assessment was mandated to rule out neurovascular compromise, clear the lung fields for traumatic pneumothorax, and initiate immediate safe immobilization prior to obtaining definitive daytime radiography.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I fell heavily on my outstretched hand, and my collarbone is incredibly sore and I can't lift my arm.
History of Present Illness: Patient provides a history of a fall onto an outstretched hand (FOOSH) occurring 2 hours ago. This resulted in focal, exquisite bony tenderness directly over the middle third of the clavicular shaft. The patient experiences pain that is significantly exacerbated by active elevation or abduction of the ipsilateral arm above 90 degrees. The patient presents supporting the weight of the injured arm with the contralateral hand to prevent painful movement. The patient forcefully denies shortness of breath, chest pain, or numbness in the fingers.
Functional Status: Holding the affected arm adducted against the body; independent in ambulation.
Social/Safety Concerns: Impaired dominant hand limits basic self-care tasks temporarily.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 134/82 | HR: 86 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert, in moderate discomfort, seated while supporting the injured elbow with the opposite hand.
Physical Exam: Shoulder examination reveals focal mid-shaft clavicle tenderness. There is mild to moderate localized soft tissue swelling obscuring the normal prominent contour of the collarbone. Crucially, there is a complete absence of a visible or palpable gross step-off deformity or tenting of the overlying skin. Lung fields are clear on auscultation bilaterally, without evidence of pneumothorax. Intact sensory and motor function in the entire upper extremity rules out brachial plexus injury. Strong radial and ulnar pulses confirm normal subclavian vascular flow.
Environmental Observations: Trip hazard (loose rug) identified as the cause of the fall; hazard removed.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Potential closed non-displaced clavicle fracture - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Displaced/Comminuted clavicle fracture - Ruled out due to: Smooth contour of the clavicle on careful palpation without severe step-off, skin tenting, or crepitus.

Traumatic pneumothorax - Ruled out due to: Bilaterally equal and clear breath sounds, oxygen saturation of 99%, and absence of respiratory distress or pleuritic chest pain.

Subclavian artery injury - Ruled out due to: Brisk distal capillary refill, strong equal radial pulses, and absence of a rapidly expanding supraclavicular hematoma.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

A fall causing isolated mid-shaft clavicle tenderness requires immediate clinical differentiation between a simple, non-displaced fracture and a complex injury threatening the subclavian vessels or lung apex. Bedside evaluation confidently confirmed an intact neurovascular axis, clear lung fields, and the absence of skin tenting or gross deformity. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically required to clear these life/limb threats and rapidly implement a broad arm sling for critical pain management and stabilization, safely bypassing an immediate emergency department visit and bridging the patient to routine daytime radiography.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Fitted the patient with a broad arm sling for strict comfort and offloading of the shoulder girdle. Prescribed scheduled oral NSAIDs and paracetamol. Explicitly advised standard anteroposterior and 15-degree cephalic tilt clavicle radiographs tomorrow to definitively confirm the suspected non-displaced fracture line.
Positive Behavioral Change: Wear the broad arm sling consistently for the next 24-48 hours. By removing the weight of the arm pulling down on the collarbone, the sling actively prevents the suspected fracture edges from grinding together, delivering massive, immediate pain relief and preventing the bone from shifting.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Broad arm sling.
Follow-up: Routine GP review tomorrow to review the x-ray results and organize formal orthopedic referral if a fracture is confirmed.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed immediately to the Emergency Department via ambulance if you develop sudden shortness of breath, sharp chest pain, if a piece of bone begins poking sharply against the skin, or if your hand becomes completely numb and cold.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: An x-ray is absolutely required tomorrow to confirm the diagnosis and verify exact bony alignment.
Clinical Course: Acute pain will persist intensely for 3-5 days. If a simple fracture is present, full healing of the collarbone typically requires 6 weeks of modified activity.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if you suddenly feel like you cannot catch your breath, or if your arm turns pale and you lose feeling in your fingers.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The collarbone acts as a strut supporting the entire arm. The sling takes over this job, allowing the injured bone to rest without bearing any weight, which stops the severe pain spasms.
Actionable Steps: Get the requested x-ray tomorrow. Sleep slightly propped up with pillows tonight for comfort. Do not attempt to lift the arm above your head.

